WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.963
Two rules turn Azure private
endpoints into something governed.

2
00:00:03.963 --> 00:00:06.049
First, forward the public zone.

3
00:00:06.049 --> 00:00:10.927
Every resolver outside Azure, on-premises,
in other clouds and at branches,

4
00:00:10.927 --> 00:00:15.005
forwards each service's public
zone, not the privatelink zone,

5
00:00:15.005 --> 00:00:19.962
to the Private Resolver's inbound
endpoint or an agency resolver that does.

6
00:00:19.962 --> 00:00:22.403
Azure Government uses different names,

7
00:00:22.403 --> 00:00:25.362
so a design spanning both
clouds needs both sets.

8
00:00:25.362 --> 00:00:30.668
Second, build the resolver in the hub as
a forwarding bridge, not an authority,

9
00:00:30.668 --> 00:00:35.888
so on-premises DNS reaches it by
conditional forwarding, never delegation.

10
00:00:35.888 --> 00:00:38.276
The slide carries the sizing limits.

11
00:00:38.276 --> 00:00:43.225
The traps are worth remembering: don't
link a ruleset to the network its own rule

12
00:00:43.225 --> 00:00:44.520
points at, or it loops,

13
00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:48.240
and don't list a custom resolver
beside Azure's built-in one.
